    {
      "id": "empire-of-paper/p1",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "In the beginning, there was no bureaucracy. Only people, memory and obligation. Early societies kept track of what mattered — land, grain, debts, people — through oral traditions and simple marks. But as communities grew, memory became fragile. What one person knew could not easily be passed to another, and with distance, time and scale, forgetting was inevitable.",
      "order": 5,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-tablets",
      "x": 101,
      "y": 34,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 45,
      "z": 1,
      "tone": "dark"
    },
    {
      "id": "empire-of-paper/p2",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "The first administrative systems emerged not from a desire to control, but from a practical need: to remember. Clay tablets in Mesopotamia, inscriptions in Egypt, and later imperial archives in Rome and China, all show the same pattern. Writing began as a tool for accounting — a way to make absence governable.^1^",
      "order": 6,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-tablets",
      "x": 101,
      "y": 84,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 40,
      "z": 1,
      "tone": "dark"
    },
    {
      "id": "empire-of-paper/img-tablet",
      "type": "image",
