    {
      "id": "empire-of-paper/p3",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "Paper changed everything. Unlike stone or clay, it was cheap, light and reproducible. It allowed information to move — across cities, regions and generations. Empires could now coordinate taxation, law, and military logistics at scales that were previously impossible.",
      "order": 10,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-tablets",
      "x": 101,
      "y": 142,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 30,
      "z": 1,
      "tone": "dark"
    },
    {
      "id": "empire-of-paper/p4",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "But paper also introduced a new problem: too much information. As archives expanded, so did the need for systems — classification, indexing, standardization. Bureaucracy was not just about writing things down; it was about making them retrievable, comparable and actionable.",
      "order": 11,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-tablets",
      "x": 101,
      "y": 177,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 35,
      "z": 1,
      "tone": "dark"
    },
