    {
      "id": "empire-of-paper/p5",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "By the time of the Ottoman Empire, paperwork connected provinces, carried orders, recorded taxes, and mediated between the center and local powers. Documents moved along postal routes, through scribes, and into archives where they could be consulted years later. This was not just record-keeping; it was a communication system, a memory machine, and a tool of legitimacy.^2^",
      "order": 17,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-tablets",
      "x": 202,
      "y": 104,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 45,
      "z": 1,
      "tone": "dark"
    },
    {
      "id": "empire-of-paper/p6",
      "type": "text",
      "variant": "body",
      "content": "Every document fixed a moment in time — a decision, a transaction, a dispute. But more importantly, it created a trace. These traces allowed states to operate beyond the presence of individuals. Authority no longer depended on personal memory; it was embedded in systems, routines and paper.",
      "order": 18,
      "relates_to": [],
      "spread_id": "s-tablets",
      "x": 202,
      "y": 154,
      "w": 79,
      "h": 35,
      "z": 1,
      "tone": "dark"
    },
